.TH NVIDIA-NSPAWN 8 "2026-11-17" "nvidia-nspawn 0.1.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
nvidia\-nspawn \- inject NVIDIA GPUs into running systemd-nspawn containers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nvidia\-nspawn plan
.BI \-\-machine " MACHINE"
.BI \-\-gpu " GPU_UUID"
.br
.B nvidia\-nspawn attach
.BI \-\-machine " MACHINE"
.BI \-\-gpu " GPU_UUID"
.br
.B nvidia\-nspawn status
.BI \-\-machine " MACHINE"
.br
.B nvidia\-nspawn verify
.BI \-\-machine " MACHINE"
.BI \-\-user " USER"
.BI \-\-cuda\-sample " PATH"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nvidia\-nspawn
runs on the host as root and injects NVIDIA device nodes and userspace driver
files into an already running
.BR systemd-nspawn (1)
container.
It uses NVIDIA Container Toolkit CDI descriptions, transfers mounts into the
existing container mount namespace, and updates the owning systemd unit's
runtime device permissions.
The container is not restarted.
.PP
Normal command results are JSON on standard output.
Failures are reported on standard error.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B plan
Inspect the container, selected GPU, CDI edits, destination paths, user mappings,
and device policy; print the proposed attachment without applying it.
Conflicting files, unsafe mount propagation, and unsupported CDI edits are
rejected.
.TP
.B attach
Apply the attachment to the current container instance.
Preserve existing device permissions and the container's own libc and loader.
Repeated successful attachment of the same GPU to the same instance does not
stack duplicate mounts.
Replacing a GPU or overwriting a previous failed attachment is not supported.
.TP
.B status
Report the recorded attachment phase and whether mounts, device nodes, and the
host driver match the attachment.
The JSON field
.B healthy
is not a GPU functional test and does not replace
.BR verify .
.TP
.B verify
Require a healthy attachment and execute GPU checks as an existing non-root
container user.
Check the user's UID, GPU UUID, driver version, and a real NVIDIA CUDA Samples
.B vectorAdd
executable.
Require a successful CUDA calculation and verify actual NVML and libcuda loading
from the injected driver files using loader diagnostics.
.PP
Verification processes join the target cgroup and namespaces and use the target
SELinux execution domain when SELinux is enabled.
CDI environment settings and supplementary groups apply only to processes
launched by the tool; they do not change persistent user group membership or
existing shells.
Other application launchers must configure any required GPU groups themselves.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-machine " MACHINE"
Name of a running, registered systemd-nspawn container.
Required for every subcommand.
.TP
.BI \-\-gpu " GPU_UUID"
Complete NVIDIA GPU UUID beginning with
.BR GPU\- .
Required for
.B plan
and
.BR attach .
Obtain UUIDs with
.BR "nvidia-smi -L" .
.TP
.BI \-\-user " USER"
Existing non-root user inside the container.
Required for
.BR verify .
.TP
.BI \-\-cuda\-sample " PATH"
Absolute container path to a real, executable NVIDIA CUDA Samples
.B vectorAdd
binary with compatible runtime dependencies.
Required for
.BR verify .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show command or subcommand help and exit.
Help does not require root.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
The host needs Python 3.11 or later, systemd, machinectl, busctl, cgroup v2,
and an already working NVIDIA driver with nvidia-smi, NVML, and libcuda.
The tested NVIDIA Container Toolkit version is 1.20.2; RPM packages require
.B nvidia-container-toolkit-base
1.20.2 or later.
The tool does not install or select a driver branch.
.PP
The kernel and libc must expose
.BR open_tree (2),
.BR move_mount (2),
and
.BR mount_setattr (2).
The mount_setattr API requires Linux 5.12 and glibc 2.36 or later.
Required privileges, namespace permissions, and filesystem support must also
be available; version numbers alone do not guarantee compatibility.
When SELinux is enabled,
.B libselinux.so.1
must be available on the host.
.PP
The container needs ldconfig, setpriv, id, readlink, and the specified ordinary
user.
For verification, prepare the CUDA Sample and its runtime dependencies in advance.
A CUDA compiler and NVIDIA Container Toolkit are not required inside the
container.
.PP
Both private user namespaces (such as nspawn \-U) and PrivateUsers=no are supported.
Private UID and GID mappings must not include host ID 0.
Without a private user namespace, systemd must be PID 1 in the container, its
system bus must be available, and the host must provide systemd 257 or newer
with systemd-run and systemctl.
Container commands and createContainer hooks run as transient services created
by the guest manager, preserving its capability, seccomp, SELinux and outer
device constraints rather than inheriting additional host privileges.
There is no privileged namespace-exec fallback.
.PP
Timeout cleanup stops the entire guest service, not merely the host
systemd-run client.
If guest processes cannot be confirmed stopped, the attachment and temporary
resources are retained and reported for explicit recovery instead of being
unmounted beneath a possibly running command.
.SH EXAMPLES
List host GPU UUIDs:
.PP
.EX
nvidia-smi -L
.EE
.PP
In the commands below, replace
.B GPU_UUID
with one complete UUID from that output, and replace the container name, user,
and Sample path with existing values:
.PP
.EX
sudo nvidia-nspawn plan --machine gpu-test --gpu GPU_UUID
sudo nvidia-nspawn attach --machine gpu-test --gpu GPU_UUID
sudo nvidia-nspawn status --machine gpu-test
sudo nvidia-nspawn verify --machine gpu-test --user developer \e
  --cuda-sample /opt/cuda-samples/vectorAdd
.EE
.PP
Open this manual or subcommand help:
.PP
.EX
man 8 nvidia-nspawn
nvidia-nspawn verify --help
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/nvidia-nspawn/MACHINE.json
Host-side runtime record of container identity, GPU, resource fingerprints,
change journal, and failure information.
This is runtime state, not a persistent container configuration.
.TP
.I /run/systemd/system.control/UNIT.d/50-DeviceAllow.conf
Systemd-managed runtime device authorization on the host.
It is not a persistent unit edit and may outlive a container restart.
.TP
.I /etc/ld.so.conf.d/nvidia-nspawn.conf
Managed driver library search directories inside the container.
The container's own ldconfig updates its loader cache.
.SH LIFECYCLE AND FAILURES
Attachments are runtime-only and do not persist across container restarts.
Driver upgrades are not applied to existing attachments automatically.
After a restart, inspect the new container instance and any remaining managed
files before attaching again; stale files are not forcibly overwritten.
.PP
No ExecStartPost hook or boot-time automatic injection is installed.
After each container start, the administrator must inspect remaining resources
and invoke attach manually.
Runtime DeviceAllow settings are not a promise of revocation on container restart;
they do not persist across a host reboot.
.PP
On attachment failure the tool attempts to undo new mounts, files, device nodes,
and device permission changes.
Cleanup failures and residual changes are reported rather than hidden.
Inspect the failure record before retrying.
Do not blindly delete recorded paths: some refer to container resources, and
existing user files must be preserved.
.PP
On timeout, supervised workers and their descendants are terminated and reaped
before rollback starts.
Waiting for an uninterruptible kernel operation may extend cleanup beyond the
command timeout; the timeout is not a guarantee of immediate return.
Completed changes are recorded incrementally.
An interruption between a filesystem operation and its progress record cannot
be treated as an atomic transaction; uncertain residual candidates are reported
without blindly deleting pre-existing resources.
.PP
No live GPU detach command or GPU memory quota configuration is provided.
Already running applications are not made to reload driver libraries.
.PP
Uninstalling the RPM is not GPU detachment or access revocation.
It removes package-owned programs and documentation but leaves runtime records,
device authorization, and injected container mounts, nodes, links, and loader
configuration or cache in place.
Existing containers may continue using the GPU after uninstall.
Administrators must separately plan access revocation and maintenance; deleting
the state record alone does not revoke access.
.SH SECURITY
Run on the host, not inside the target container.
The tool does not disable SELinux, change host device ownership or modes,
modify Secure Boot, reload drivers, or restart containers.
It does not bypass parent cgroup restrictions or SELinux policy.
.PP
Host mounts are made private and read-only, including recursive restrictions for
rbind trees, while still detached and before becoming visible to the container.
.PP
Ensure that imported container root filesystems have appropriate SELinux file
labels.
Relabel only the container's backing directory, not a live
.I /proc/LEADER/root
view containing host bind mounts, to avoid relabeling host driver files.
.PP
The Fedora validation used SELinux enforcing with the standard nspawn process
domain
.BR unconfined_service_t .
It does not establish compatibility with custom confined domains such as
.BR container_t .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed, or help was displayed.
For
.BR status ,
this does not imply that
.B healthy
is true; inspect the JSON result.
.TP
.B 1
An operation, permission check, prerequisite, or verification failed.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line usage was invalid, such as a missing required argument.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd-nspawn (1),
.BR machinectl (1),
.BR busctl (1),
.BR setpriv (1),
.BR ldconfig (8),
.BR nvidia-smi (1)
